## Context
Every gaze fork hand-writes its own collection loop over `.panel/<pr>/`
(`skills/gaze/references/fork-contract.md`, `panel.md` step 3): a fork lives
only while it calls tools, so the wait must be a tool call, and the recipe
leaves its shape to each session. Measured 2026-10-28 over 14 days of Claude
Code transcripts (3379 files): sleep-poll loops are the largest slow-call
class, and `.panel/` waits are most of them (211 of 280 on 2026-10-27, 75 of
106 on 2026-10-28; 1216's instruction route merged 2026-10-26 did not lower
the count). Variants seen: `for i in $(seq 1 55)`, `until [ -f a ] && [ -f b ]`,
`while [ $SECONDS -lt $end ]`, inline Python `time.sleep`, some running into
the 600 s tool cap and re-issued (one loop shape repeated 28 times within
single sessions). Durations are upper bounds (tool_use to tool_result).

Codex, same window (656 sessions): only 14 shell `.panel` waits. Agent waits
mostly go through the native `wait_agent` (36 sessions use only it); 14
sessions fall back to `sleep` then `list_agents`. Its other native `sleep`
calls (2675 of 45-60 s, ~34.8 h requested) wait on shell jobs (`tail`, a
`python3` check), the class this helper serves.

Pi, same window: interactive sessions are 8, with 137 shell calls, none slow
and no `.panel` wait. Arena runs (647 headless sessions) hold 41 poll loops
(~2 h) and 151 `sleep N; check` calls (~6.6 h); they are the measured
benchmark, so this ticket does not touch them.

The helper is a plain script, so it serves Claude Code, Codex and Pi alike;
it is not a guard (0976's burden of proof is not engaged).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] One helper (e.g. `scripts/wait-roster.py <dir> --roster FILE|NAMES
      --deadline S`) blocks until every roster name exists as `<name>.md`
      (never `.part`), or the deadline passes; stays under the runtime's
      per-call cap and says so; exit 0 all present, 1 deadline with the
      missing names listed, 2 usage
- [ ] `fork-contract.md` and `panel.md` step 3 (and any other skill that
      collects seat files, e.g. verify-adherence) call it instead of
      describing a hand-written loop; the 15-minute gaze deadline is honoured
      across re-issued calls
- [ ] Tests: all-present, deadline with a missing name, `.part` ignored
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine (raid 534413)
Premise holds (2026-10-28): nothing blocks on `.panel/`. `scripts/seat_clock.py`
`roster`/`plan-check` (l.130, l.162) are non-blocking snapshots; the recipes at
`skills/gaze/references/panel.md:72-78`, `fork-contract.md:15-34` and
`skills/verify-adherence/SKILL.md:229-241` still describe a hand-written loop.
Simplest path: a stdlib `scripts/wait-roster.py <dir> (--roster FILE | NAME...)`
with an absolute `--until EPOCH` deadline, `.md` only (never `.part`), the
manifest format parsed through `seat_clock.manifest_items`, not re-parsed.
How details implied by criteria 1-2 (not new intent): a configurable slice
under the runtime's per-call cap (Claude Code Bash 600 s) ending with its own
exit code (3: slice ended, deadline not reached, re-issue); the deadline
carried across re-issues as the same absolute `--until`; a stale report from an
earlier round must not count (`--newer-than MANIFEST`, or callers delete it
first, as verify-adherence does; pick one and test it); a test for `--until`
already past. Keep it roster-only: no general wait tool, no inotify, no JSON.
Codex agent waits stay on native `wait_agent`; the helper serves shell waits.

## Blind-spot (raid 534413, gpt-6-luna, OpenAI)
- Kept, for the author (outside the exit criteria): `scripts/trace-friction.py`
  counts poll loops by command text, so `wait-roster.py` calls would drop out
  of the measured series without less waiting. Counting helper calls as wait
  commands keeps the Monitor step honest; not done in this ticket unless asked.
- Dismissed: `runtime.py wake arm` instead of a blocking wait. A gaze fork ends
  when it stops calling tools (`fork-contract.md`), so it cannot idle for a wake.
- Covered: consumer inventory (Imagine grep; the executor greps once more).
